---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = '/misc/hotels/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Rooms with a View — Huanyu Chen"
  description="The hotels and resorts we liked most from five years of trips, from a Mauna Lani atrium and one hotel a night in Waikīkī to high floors over Times Square, the Strip, Dallas, Chicago and Sydney.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2026</p>
  <h1>Rooms with a View</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">The hotels we would happily stay at again, and what we saw from them.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>25 hotels · 2022–2026</strong><br />
    5 hotels in 5 nights in Waikīkī · 7 resorts · 12 city hotels, most of them high up · 1 cabin in the woods<br />
    Highest room: the 43rd floor in Sydney · best New Year's Eve: right above Times Square
  </div>

  <p>
    On 小红书, people share the hotels they stayed at the way others share restaurants: the room, the view, the
    pool, and whether it was worth it. Going through five years of photos for the other posts on this page, I found
    that I had been doing half of that without noticing. Almost every trip has a few photos taken from a hotel
    window, usually on the first evening, before unpacking.
  </p>
  <p>
    So here are the hotels we liked, out of the eighty or so we have slept in since 2022. Most of the rest were
    one-night stops on long drives, the kind of place you remember only for its waffle machine. These are the
    ones worth a detour, sorted into three groups: one hotel a night in Waikīkī, resorts, and city hotels, where
    the reason to stay was usually the view from a high floor. None of this is sponsored; quite a few of the city
    nights were booked with hotel points.
  </p>

  <h2>Waikīkī, one hotel a night</h2>
  <p>
    On Oʻahu in September 2026 we tried something we had never done before: a different hotel every night. Waikīkī
    is small, and it turned five nights into a tasting menu.
    Here they are in the order I would recommend them.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Ka Laʻi Waikiki Beach, LXR</h3>
  <p>
    The best of the five, and the quietest. Our balcony faced west over the green lawns of Fort DeRussy, so the
    sunset dropped straight into the ocean in front of us, with the towers of Waikīkī lined up along the shore. At
    7:45 that Friday evening, the balcony gave us front-row seats for the fireworks from Hilton Hawaiian Village's
    luau, bursting over the beach next door.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-fireworks.jpg'} alt="Fireworks bursting red, green and gold over the beach at night, with pink fireworks rising from the shore, a lit boat on the water and hotel towers at the right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Friday fireworks from the balcony, 7:45 pm (a frame from a video).</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Before that came the sunset, and in the morning the same view was all park, palms and blue water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-sunset.jpg'} alt="Sunset over the ocean from a glass-railed balcony, with Waikīkī hotel towers along the shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset from the balcony, 6:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-morning.jpg'} alt="Morning view over the green park of Fort DeRussy to hotel towers and the ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The next morning, over Fort DeRussy.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is also an infinity pool above the palms, and the bathroom has a deep marble soaking tub.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-pool.jpg'} alt="The edge of an infinity pool with a glass railing, palms and treetops beyond, and a tower at the left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pool, 9:31 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-tub.jpg'} alt="A deep white soaking tub set in gray-veined marble, with a wooden bath tray and rolled towels." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The soaking tub.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>2. Hilton Hawaiian Village</h3>
  <p>
    The classic. It is a whole village of towers at the west end of the beach, with a lagoon and pools, and our
    room was high enough to see the full curve of Waikīkī Beach all the way to Diamond Head.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhv-panorama.jpg'} alt="High view over Waikīkī: hotel towers, pools, the beach curving east and Diamond Head on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The whole beach, and Diamond Head.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhv-balcony.jpg'} alt="A shaded balcony with two chairs, framing the towers, the curve of the beach, turquoise water and Diamond Head." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The balcony, in the morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Ambassador Waikiki</h3>
  <p>
    The surprise. The Ambassador is a small Tapestry hotel a few blocks back from the beach, and our room had a
    double-decker bed: compact, cozy and a bit like a college dorm. My wife liked it a lot, more than some of the
    much grander rooms on this list. We did not take a single photo there, so the pictures below are the hotel's own.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ambassador-bunk-room.jpg'} alt="A compact hotel room: a bed below a wooden bunk with a ladder, a window onto city towers, a wall-mounted TV, two round leather stools and a slatted wooden wardrobe." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bunk-bed room.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ambassador-bunk-close.jpg'} alt="Close-up of the wooden bunk bed with white bedding, a ladder and a reading light, beside a window and pink leaf-patterned wallpaper." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bunk, ladder and reading light. Photos: Ambassador Waikiki (hotel website).</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>4. Embassy Suites Waikiki Beach Walk</h3>
  <p>
    A solid suite in the middle of everything, and the best sunrise of the trip: at 6:36 on our last morning the
    clouds over the rooftops turned gold.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'embassy-waikiki-dawn.jpg'} alt="Dawn over Waikīkī rooftops and towers from a balcony, with gold clouds above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dawn from the balcony.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>5. DoubleTree by Hilton Alana</h3>
  <p>
    Last on my list, but not for the view. Our room was high up, facing inland over central Waikīkī rather than the
    sea, and at night the towers around it were lit window by window, a vertical city of tiny yellow rooms, with
    the streets and pool decks glowing far below. It was a great view.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'alana-towers.jpg'} alt="Waikīkī towers lit up at night, with a street and a glowing pool far below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the room, 9:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'alana-night.jpg'} alt="Night view from a balcony over lit hotel towers and a pool deck below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same balcony, a few seconds later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Resorts</h2>

  <h3>Mauna Lani, Auberge Resorts · Big Island, 2025</h3>
  <p>
    The most beautiful building on this list. The hotel is built around a huge open-air atrium filled with a
    garden, and our ocean-view balcony looked straight down the beach and over the palms to the sea. I wrote about
    the design in the <a href="/misc/big-island-2025">Big Island post</a>; I would recommend it to anyone visiting
    the Kohala Coast.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maunalani-balcony.jpg'} alt="An ocean-view balcony framed by an overhang: blue sea, a beach and palms." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The balcony, 11 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maunalani-atrium.jpg'} alt="Looking down into the open-air atrium: terraced balconies, palms and a wooden walkway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The atrium.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Hilton Waikoloa Village · Big Island, 2026</h3>
  <p>
    A resort big enough to need a tram and a canal boat, with a gallery of statues along its walkways and a
    balcony that looked over lawns and lagoons to the ocean. One morning a bright green gecko sat on the balcony wall.
    At night the lagoons light up. More in the
    <a href="/misc/hawaii-2026">Hawaiʻi 2026 post</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-balcony.jpg'} alt="Balcony view over landscaped lawns and lagoons to the ocean on a sunny morning." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view, 10 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-night.jpg'} alt="Night view from a balcony over the lit resort grounds and lagoons." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same view after midnight.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Hyatt Ziva Cancún · 2022</h3>
  <p>
    An all-inclusive at the tip of the Cancún hotel zone. The room looked down on
    a stack of blue pools, a palapa and the Caribbean, and the infinity pools by the lobby seem to run straight
    into the sea.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ziva-room-view.jpg'} alt="High view over blue pools, a lawn and a palapa to the Caribbean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the room.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ziva-infinity.jpg'} alt="Infinity pools under a canopy, with palms and the sea beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The infinity pools.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Zachari Dunes on Mandalay Beach · Oxnard, 2025</h3>
  <p>
    The easiest escape from Los Angeles on this list: an hour up the coast, a Spanish-style courtyard with a rock
    waterfall, and a balcony over red tile roofs to the dunes and the ocean.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zachari-balcony.jpg'} alt="View from a balcony over red tile roof to sand dunes, palms and the ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the balcony, 8:37 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zachari-courtyard.jpg'} alt="A courtyard path with succulent beds and a rock waterfall between red-roofed wings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The courtyard.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Westin Princeville Ocean Resort Villas · Kauaʻi, 2026</h3>
  <p>
    On Kauaʻi's north shore, a lagoon-style pool under enormous clouds and a stream full of koi below a small
    waterfall.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'princeville-lagoon-pool.jpg'} alt="A still lagoon-style pool reflecting palms and white clouds, with thatched cabanas and loungers beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pool, 8:21 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'princeville-koi.jpg'} alt="Orange and white koi in a stream below a rock waterfall and a lawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The koi stream.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Our room was one of the resort's villas, with a vaulted wooden ceiling and a screened lanai.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'princeville-villa.jpg'} alt="A villa living room with a vaulted white wooden ceiling, ceiling fans, white sofas, a kitchen at the far end and a screened lanai along one side." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The villa.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Hotel St. Lauren · Catalina, 2024 &nbsp;and&nbsp; The Wilde · Sedona, 2026</h3>
  <p>
    Two smaller places. The St. Lauren sits on the hill above Avalon, and its balconies look over the town's
    rooftops to the harbor. The Wilde, in Sedona, is a resort of stucco wings and terraced red-walled gardens with
    the red rocks behind.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stlauren-harbor.jpg'} alt="Avalon from the hill: white buildings and palms above the harbor, with a cruise ship anchored offshore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Avalon and the harbor from the hill.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wilde-garden.jpg'} alt="Terraced red-walled garden stairs with desert plants beside a stucco wing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The gardens at the Wilde.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>City hotels, high up</h2>
  <p>
    In a city, the room I want is the one on the highest floor I can get, and these are the ones where it paid off.
  </p>

  <h3>Hotel Edison · New York, New Year's Eve 2022</h3>
  <p>
    Hard to beat for location. Our window looked straight down on Times Square, so on New Year's Eve we
    watched the crowds, the stage and the billboards from above,.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'edison-times-square.jpg'} alt="Looking straight down from a hotel window onto the billboards and stage area of Times Square at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Times Square from the room, 8:42 pm on December 31.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Palazzo, ARIA and Caesars Palace · Las Vegas</h3>
  <p>
    Las Vegas rooms are mostly about the view out of them. The Palazzo gave us a suite in 2022, with the Venetian
    tower and the Strip below the window.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'palazzo-view.jpg'} alt="High view down onto the Venetian tower and the Strip in daylight." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Palazzo, 2022: the Venetian below.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'palazzo-suite.jpg'} alt="A suite living room with dark red curtains, a round table, a desk and a sofa in front of a large window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The suite.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In 2024 we were back at the Palazzo for a night, in a room facing the Sphere, and the next night at Caesars
    Palace, where the Garden of the Gods pools look like a Roman ruin with sun loungers.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'palazzo-sphere.jpg'} alt="The Sphere lit up at night, seen from a high hotel room, with the city lights behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Palazzo, 2024: the Sphere at midnight.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-pools.jpg'} alt="High view over the Caesars Palace pools: a domed temple, round pools, red loungers and statues." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Caesars Palace: the Garden of the Gods.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And at ARIA, a high floor looked straight at Vdara next door and down onto the pool deck.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'aria-vdara.jpg'} alt="The curved blue glass Vdara tower beside the city and mountains, seen from a high room." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>ARIA: Vdara next door.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'aria-pool.jpg'} alt="Looking straight down onto a pool deck with teal umbrellas and a curved entry drive." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>ARIA's pool, from above.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Hyatt Regency Dallas · 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Next to Reunion Tower, and the view of the downtown skyline is the best thing about it, especially at night,
    when one tower is outlined in green.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-night.jpg'} alt="Dallas skyline at night with a tower outlined in green lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Night, 9:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-day.jpg'} alt="Downtown Dallas towers in morning light from a high room." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The next morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Sable at Navy Pier · Chicago, 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The one I would recommend most from our <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">2024 road trip</a>. Most nights on that
    trip were in budget hotels along the highway, to save money for the drive. For Chicago we redeemed points for
    the Sable, a hotel on Navy Pier itself, and it was worth every one: the window looks back across the water at
    the whole Chicago skyline, with the cruise boats right below. The bathroom had backlit mirrors and a wood-tiled
    shower.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sable-skyline.jpg'} alt="The Chicago skyline across the water from Navy Pier, with a cruise boat below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The skyline from the room, 9:07 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sable-bath.jpg'} alt="A hotel bathroom with two backlit round-cornered mirrors and a wood-tiled glass shower." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bathroom.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Hilton Sydney · 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The highest room on this list: the 43rd floor, in the middle of the city. At sunset the towers lit up below us,
    and at six in the morning there was a window seat to sit in and watch them wake up. (The four nights before
    that, at the Novotel, were less memorable; my advisor, at the Sofitel next door, had a stunning view of the
    harbor.)
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sydney-sunset.jpg'} alt="Sunset over the lit towers of central Sydney from a 43rd-floor window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset from the 43rd floor.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sydney-nook.jpg'} alt="A deep window seat framing city towers in morning light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The window seat, 6:39 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Vancouver and Denver</h3>
  <p>
    Two conference cities, three good windows. In Vancouver, the Hilton downtown looked down a street of glass
    towers to the white roof of BC Place. In Denver, the Embassy Suites by the convention center had the downtown
    towers.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vancouver-bcplace.jpg'} alt="High view over Vancouver towers to the white roof of BC Place." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hilton Vancouver Downtown, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-embassy.jpg'} alt="Downtown Denver towers and rooftops in morning light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Embassy Suites Denver, 2024.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two years later, on a spring-break drive, the Art Hotel Denver looked over the art museum's roof garden to the
    Rocky Mountains.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arthotel-mountains.jpg'} alt="A museum roof garden and city buildings with the Rocky Mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Art Hotel Denver, 2026.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>With character</h2>
  <p>
    A few places earn a mention for something other than the view: the Hyatt Regency San Francisco's tiered atrium,
    seen from the top floors at midnight, and the Four Seasons Austin's quiet courtyard of old trees.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hyattsf-atrium.jpg'} alt="Looking down a tall tiered hotel atrium onto a lit bar and a large sculpture." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hyatt Regency San Francisco.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fsaustin-courtyard.jpg'} alt="A courtyard of large trees below hotel towers in Austin." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Four Seasons Austin.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And Postcard Cabins near Big Bear: a tiny black cabin in the pines with a big picture window over the bed. Our
    dog came too.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'postcard-cabin.jpg'} alt="A small black cabin with a big picture window, among pine trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Postcard Cabins, Big Bear.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>What I look for now</h2>
  <p>
    After eighty-odd hotels, my rule is simple: a high floor in a city, a balcony at a resort, and a window I want
    to photograph before I unpack. The rooms blur together after a while; the photos from the windows don't.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Hotels listed from my own photos, booking records and notes; times are local. Photo
    locations have been removed from the published images. No hotel paid for or asked for this post.</small></p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Some photos in this post are my wife's.</small></p>
